Ticketmaster is one of the most popular platforms for buying, selling, and managing event tickets. But what happens when you can't attend an event? Whether you're curious about how much Ticketmaster charges to sell tickets or want to know how to sell your tickets back to Ticketmaster, this guide has everything you need.
Understanding Ticketmaster's Resale Options
Ticketmaster provides a simple and secure resale option that allows users to sell their tickets to other fans directly on its platform. This service eliminates the need to use third-party sites and ensures a seamless transfer process.
Ticketmaster also has specific events where users can sell tickets back to the platform via a buyback program. However, this feature isn’t available for all tickets.
How Much Does Ticketmaster Charge to Sell Tickets?
When you sell tickets on Ticketmaster’s resale platform, the company charges a service fee. Here’s a breakdown:
1. Seller Fees
•	Ticketmaster typically charges 10% to 15% of the ticket’s sale price as a seller fee.
•	For example, if your ticket sells for $100, you might pay $10–$15 in fees.
2. Buyer Fees
•	Buyers also pay a fee, which can range from 15% to 20% of the ticket price.
•	These fees ensure secure transactions, platform maintenance, and customer support.
3. Why Fees Matter
These fees may seem high, but they provide peace of mind for both buyers and sellers by guaranteeing authenticity and a secure payment process.
How to Sell Your Tickets Back to Ticketmaster
There are two primary ways to resell tickets on Ticketmaster: through their resale marketplace or via a ticket buyback program.
Option 1: Using Ticketmaster’s Resale Marketplace
The resale marketplace is the most common option for selling tickets on Ticketmaster. Here’s how it works:
Step 1: Log In to Your Account
•	Visit Ticketmaster.com and log in to your account.
•	Go to the “My Tickets” section.
Step 2: Select the Event
•	Choose the event for which you want to sell tickets.
•	If the resale option is available, you’ll see a “Sell” button next to your tickets.
Step 3: Set Your Price
•	Ticketmaster allows you to set your ticket price.
•	You can use Ticketmaster’s suggested pricing tool or manually enter your desired price.
Step 4: Confirm and List Your Tickets
•	Once you’ve entered the details, confirm the listing.
•	Your tickets will be made available for purchase on Ticketmaster’s marketplace.
Step 5: Get Paid
•	Once your tickets sell, the funds will be deposited into your account. Payment is usually processed 5–7 business days after the event.
Option 2: Selling Tickets Back to Ticketmaster Through Buyback Programs
For select events, Ticketmaster may offer a buyback program where you can sell your tickets directly to them.
Step 1: Check Availability
•	Not all tickets are eligible for buyback. Visit your “My Tickets” section and see if there’s an option to sell tickets back to Ticketmaster.
Step 2: Accept the Buyback Offer
•	Ticketmaster will offer a fixed amount for your tickets.
•	If you agree, accept the offer to initiate the transaction.
Step 3: Complete the Process
•	Follow the on-screen prompts to finalize the sale.
•	Payment is typically issued shortly after the buyback is complete.
Tips for Selling Tickets on Ticketmaster
1. Price Your Tickets Competitively
•	Research the market to understand the demand for your event.
•	Use Ticketmaster’s pricing tools to gauge a fair price.
2. List Your Tickets Early
•	The earlier you list your tickets, the more time you’ll have to find a buyer.
•	Tickets closer to the event date may sell for lower prices due to reduced demand.
3. Ensure Tickets Are Transferable
•	Not all tickets are eligible for resale. Check the event’s policies before listing your tickets.
4. Be Aware of Fees
•	Factor in the seller fees when setting your ticket price to ensure you get a fair payout.
5. Respond Quickly to Sales
•	If you need to manually transfer tickets, do so promptly to maintain a good seller rating.
What Happens If Your Tickets Don’t Sell?
If your tickets don’t sell on Ticketmaster, you have a few options:
1. Lower the Price
•	Adjust the ticket price to make it more appealing to buyers.
2. Use Other Resale Platforms
•	Consider listing your tickets on other platforms like StubHub or Vivid Seats.
3. Gift or Donate the Tickets
•	If you’re unable to sell the tickets, giving them to friends, family, or a charitable organization is a great alternative.
Pros and Cons of Selling Tickets Back to Ticketmaster
Pros:
•	Secure Transactions: The platform guarantees authenticity and payment.
•	Wide Audience: Ticketmaster attracts millions of buyers worldwide.
•	Convenience: Integrated tools make it easy to list and sell tickets.
Cons:
•	Fees: Seller fees can eat into your profits.
•	Limited Buyback Options: Not all events or tickets are eligible for direct buyback.
FAQs About Selling Tickets on Ticketmaster
1. Can I Sell All Tickets on Ticketmaster?
Not all tickets are eligible for resale. Check your ticket details to confirm.
2. How Long Does It Take to Get Paid?
Payment is processed 5–7 business days after the event concludes.
3. Are Fees Refundable If My Tickets Don’t Sell?
No, fees are only charged once the tickets are sold.
4. What Happens If My Tickets Are Non-Transferable?
Non-transferable tickets cannot be sold on Ticketmaster. Contact customer support for guidance.
Conclusion
Selling tickets back to Ticketmaster is a straightforward process, whether you use their resale marketplace or a buyback program. While fees are a part of the process, the platform offers a secure and reliable way to connect with buyers. By following the steps outlined above and keeping pricing competitive, you can ensure a successful sale.
If you’re unable to sell on Ticketmaster, consider alternative platforms or donating your tickets. With a little effort and planning, you’ll make the most of your unused tickets!

